# level7 → level8

The program allocates two `{id, data}` nodes and separate eight-byte data
buffers. `strcpy(first->data, argv[1])` can overflow into the following
node. In the VM, `second->data` is 20 bytes after `first->data`. Replace it
with the writable `puts` GOT slot at `0x08049928`. The second `strcpy` then
writes the address of `m` (`0x080484f4`) into that slot.

After these copies, the program reads `/home/user/level8/.pass` into global
`c` and calls `puts("~~")`. Because `puts` now points to `m`, that call
prints `c` and the current time. `m` was already present in the binary; the
exploit redirects the existing call to it.

From the level7 account on the RainFall VM:

```sh
./level7 \
  "$(python2.7 -c 'import struct; print "A"*20 + struct.pack("<I",0x08049928)')" \
  "$(python2.7 -c 'import struct; print struct.pack("<I",0x080484f4)')"
```

Use `disassemble main` to follow both copies and the later `fgets`/`puts`;
`info address m` and `objdump -R ./level7` give the two addresses. The
password before the timestamp is recorded in `flag`.
